  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 231 AM CDT Sat Aug 29 2026\n\nThe mid/upper ridge over the Southern Rockies is progged to \ntranslate eastward over the weekend bringing rising \nheights/increasing thickness and rising temperatures to the area \nonce again. The airmass is expected to remain capped with most \nlocations remaining dry. A small cluster of showers and \nthunderstorms over northwest Kansas was drifting eastward across \nnorthern Kansas along the eastern periphery of the LLJ. This activity \nis expected to remain north of our forecast area early in the \nperiod before dissipating. A capping inversion should preclude \nany storm activity this afternoon and tonight and again on \nSunday. Breezy southerly winds and a deeply mixed pbl will \nresult in lower humidity this weekend, similar to yesterday \nwhich could help boost temperatures into the 102-105 range for \nmuch of the area. As we move into Sunday night, some storm \nactivity may develop over the high plains of western Kansas \nwhile drifting eastward Sunday night. Some of this activity \ncould approach our central KS counties before dissipating.\n\nHot and breezy conditions with mostly dry weather conditions are \nexpected to persist through much if not all of the upcoming week as \nthe mid/upper ridge translates eastward through the middle of the \nweek before building back into the Southern and Central Plains later \nin the week. A persistence type forecast is anticipated with triple \ndigit heat and some record high temperatures possible at times.",
